    stories: [
      { t: 'The Translator', p: 'When Mrs. Petrova first arrived at the clinic, she spoke no English, and the receptionist had already sighed twice. Fifteen-year-old Daniel, waiting with his little sister, recognized the Bulgarian his grandmother spoke. He hesitated. He hated being noticed. But Mrs. Petrova\'s hands were shaking as she tried to explain the pain in her chest, and the nurse kept repeating the same question louder, as if volume could cross languages. Daniel stood. "She says it started last night," he translated, his voice cracking. "And it gets worse when she climbs stairs." The nurse\'s face changed instantly. Within minutes, Mrs. Petrova was in an exam room. Later, she found Daniel in the waiting room and pressed a wrapped pastry into his hands. She said only one word, but he understood it perfectly.',
        q: ['What internal conflict does Daniel face?|his shyness versus his wish to help|whether to leave the clinic|whether to learn Bulgarian', 'What does "as if volume could cross languages" suggest?|speaking louder does not help someone understand a language|the nurse was helpful|the room was noisy', 'Why does the nurse\'s face change?|she realizes the symptoms are serious|she is angry at Daniel|she speaks Bulgarian', 'What is implied by the final sentence?|Mrs. Petrova said "thank you" in Bulgarian|she said goodbye forever|Daniel misunderstood her', 'What is the theme?|courage can mean stepping forward despite fear|clinics are crowded|pastries are delicious'] },
      { t: 'The Science of Awe', p: 'Psychologists define awe as the feeling we get in the presence of something vast that challenges our understanding: a starry sky, a thundering waterfall, a symphony. For a long time, awe was considered too fuzzy to study. Recently, however, researchers have found that it has measurable effects. In one experiment, people who spent a minute gazing up at towering eucalyptus trees were more likely to help a stranger who "accidentally" dropped some pens than people who looked at a plain building. Other studies suggest awe can shrink our sense of self in a healthy way, making daily worries feel smaller and connection to others feel larger. The implications are practical: a walk in nature or a night spent stargazing may be more than a pleasant break. It may make us better neighbors.',
        q: ['How do psychologists define awe?|a feeling in the presence of something vast that challenges understanding|a type of fear|happiness after winning', 'What did the eucalyptus experiment suggest?|awe may make people more helpful|trees make people tired|buildings are more inspiring', 'Why does the author put "accidentally" in quotation marks?|the pens were dropped on purpose as part of the test|the researcher was clumsy|it was a real accident', 'What does "shrink our sense of self in a healthy way" mean?|make personal worries feel smaller|make people physically smaller|make people less confident', 'What is the author\'s purpose?|to explain research on awe and its benefits|to argue against science|to tell a personal story'] },
      { t: 'A Letter from 1863', p: 'My dearest Margaret, the river here is wide and brown, and the nights are colder than I imagined. We march at dawn and rest at dusk, and I confess my boots have more holes than leather. Yet I write not to burden you but to tell you of a kindness. Yesterday, an old farmer whose fence we had crossed came out with a basket of apples for every man in our company. He asked nothing in return. When the captain offered payment, the farmer shook his head and said he had a son somewhere far away, and he hoped someone was feeding him too. I think of that often. Kiss the children for me, and tell little Thomas I have kept the button he gave me. Your loving husband, James.',
        q: ['What type of source is this letter?|a primary source|a secondary source|a textbook', 'Why does the farmer give away apples?|he hopes someone is kind to his own son far away|he wants to be paid|the captain ordered him', 'What does the detail about James\'s boots suggest?|conditions are hard for the soldiers|James is very wealthy|it is summer', 'What is James\'s main purpose in writing?|to share a moment of kindness and comfort his family|to complain about the army|to describe the river\'s history', 'What does keeping the button suggest?|James treasures his connection to his family|buttons are valuable|Thomas is a soldier'] },
      { t: 'Should Homework Be Graded?', p: 'Some schools have stopped grading homework, arguing that practice should be a safe place to make mistakes. Under traditional systems, a student who struggles at first but masters the material by test day may still end up with a low grade because of early homework scores. Supporters of the change say this punishes the learning process itself. Critics respond that without grades, many students simply won\'t do the work, and that homework teaches responsibility and time management. Early reports are mixed: some teachers see more honest effort, since copying answers no longer "pays," while others see completion rates drop. Perhaps the real question is not whether to grade homework, but how to make it meaningful enough that students want to do it.',
        q: ['What is the main argument for not grading homework?|practice should be a safe place to make mistakes|homework is too easy|teachers are too busy', 'What counterargument do critics make?|students may stop doing the work|homework is too hard|grades are confusing', 'Why might copying "no longer pay"?|ungraded homework gives no grade reward for copying|students are paid for homework|copying is allowed', 'What does the final sentence suggest?|making homework meaningful matters more than grading it|homework should be banned|all homework should be graded', 'How does the author present the issue?|evenhandedly, with evidence on both sides|as a one-sided rant|as a fictional story'] },
    ],
